            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 28,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Milton D. Miller II",
                        "email": "mdmii@outlook.com",
                        "username": "mdmillerii"
                    },
                    "message": "i remember all too many times of prior systems getting stuck in a transition state requiring requiring a power disruption or similar drastic behavior.\n\noften it was a crash of the host while a soft power off had been requested, rejecting a hard power off request.\n\nyou haven't said what failures you are observing,  is it impatience causing power on to fail because it's still powering off?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 28,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, the primary use case is someone not waiting for a power off before they start a power on(POWER based servers have hw procedures they run during power off, and SPI mux configurations)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 28,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 36,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Milton D. Miller II",
                        "email": "mdmii@outlook.com",
                        "username": "mdmillerii"
                    },
                    "message": "how can you ensure the transition I never stable?\n\ninterfere with the host while requesting a soft power off \n\nwhy should a potential host state interfere with chassis transition requests?\n\ntest a hard power off when a soft power off had been requested."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 36,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "This host and chassis states are very intertwined. One valid use case is what you describe, the user has started a soft power off (wait for host firmware to properly shutdown) and they decide they do not want to wait so they issue a chassisoff to just skip to the hard power off. This will now require the user pass in the --force. The goal with this commit is to keep things very simple, obmcutil could quickly get out of control if it really starts trying to enforce state machine transitions."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Shawn McCarney",
                        "email": "shawnmm@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "smccarney"
                    },
                    "message": "Appreciate this change; I think this will be very beneficial."
                },
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 90,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Milton D. Miller II",
                        "email": "mdmii@outlook.com",
                        "username": "mdmillerii"
                    },
                    "message": "force a comment though what?\n\nforce issuing the command ignoring preconditions."
                },
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 90,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 300,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Shawn McCarney",
                        "email": "shawnmm@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "smccarney"
                    },
                    "message": "I worry that when people initially hit this, they will read the suggestion and blindly add `--force` to their automated test scripts.\n\nShould this warn that use of force is an unsupported use case and can lead to unexpected errors and behavior?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 300,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Milton D. Miller II",
                        "email": "mdmii@outlook.com",
                        "username": "mdmillerii"
                    },
                    "message": "how about \n use --force to override."
                },
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 300,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Yeah, I agree. I added the \"to override\" to make it clear this is an override. I also added a \"use with caution\" to the --help."
                }
            ],
